    "modelAnswer": "An effective backup strategy must allow the company to recover all of its data quickly after loss, corruption or a cyber attack such as ransomware, ideally with copies kept securely off-site.\n\nThe current strategy has several strengths. Taking disk images of all servers, PCs and laptops is effective because it allows a full (bare-metal) restore of an entire machine, not just its files, which speeds up recovery after a serious failure. The weekly copy of all company data to a portable drive acts as a full backup, while the daily copies of working files to USB sticks act like incremental backups, giving more recent recovery points between the weekly copies. RAID 1 mirrors data across drives so that if one server drive fails, the system keeps running with no downtime.\n\nHowever, there are significant weaknesses. RAID 1 is not actually a backup — it only protects against hardware failure. If a file is accidentally deleted or encrypted by ransomware, the change is mirrored instantly onto both drives, so the data is still lost. A more serious problem is that all of the backup media appear to be kept on-site. A single event such as a fire, flood or theft could destroy the servers and the USB/portable backups together, leaving no way to recover. Reusing only five USB sticks means the company has at most five days of file history, so older versions are overwritten and cannot be retrieved. There is also no mention of encryption, so a lost or stolen USB stick or portable drive would expose company data, breaching GDPR. Finally, there is no evidence that the backups are ever tested by restoring them, so they may not actually work when needed.\n\nOverall, the strategy is only partially effective: it provides good recovery points and full machine images, but the lack of an off-site or cloud copy is a critical flaw that could result in total data loss. The most important improvement would be to add an encrypted off-site or cloud backup and to test restores regularly, ensuring the company can always recover even after a site-wide disaster."
