    "modelAnswer": "WPA2 and WPA3 are both wireless security standards that encrypt the traffic between devices and a wireless access point, so that data cannot be read if it is intercepted.\n\nWPA3 is the more secure of the two. It uses stronger encryption and a more secure handshake that provides much better protection against offline password-guessing and brute-force attacks, so even a relatively weak Wi-Fi password is harder to crack. WPA3 also offers individualised encryption on open networks, which would benefit the company's guest Wi-Fi by protecting each guest's traffic from other users on the same network. Using WPA3 on the staff WAP would therefore give the strongest protection for sensitive internal traffic.\n\nWPA2, by contrast, is older and has known vulnerabilities, such as the KRACK attack, which can allow an attacker to intercept traffic in certain conditions. However, WPA2 still provides solid encryption and is far better than an unsecured network. Its main advantage is compatibility: virtually every wireless device supports WPA2, whereas some older laptops, phones and IoT devices may not support WPA3 at all. If the company forced WPA3 only, those older devices — particularly guest devices it does not control — might be unable to connect.\n\nThere is therefore a trade-off between security and compatibility. The strongest approach is to use WPA3 on the WAPs wherever the connecting devices support it, as this gives the best protection for both staff and guests. Where legacy devices must be supported, a mixed WPA2/WPA3 ('transition') mode can be used as a fallback, accepting slightly weaker security in exchange for compatibility. Overall, WPA3 should be the default choice for the company, with WPA2 retained only where it is genuinely needed for older equipment."
